<div class="sans-serif editor-aside">
  <h3>React for structure, imperative for the drag</h3>
  <p>
    The whole editor is a React app — accordion steps, thumbnails, portals, the lot.
    That worked until I dragged a control point: every <span class="monospace">pointermove</span>
    re-rendered a large subtree (preview SVG, indicators, sidebar), and the frame rate fell apart.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the hot path skips React’s render loop. On drag, handlers mutate
    <span class="monospace">MarkerPart.controlPoints</span> directly, then:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Push circle / cross positions with CSS <span class="monospace">translate</span> on
      DOM refs — no React commit.
    </li>
    <li>
      Rebuild the SVG layers through <span class="monospace">imperativeReactComponent</span>:
      each step owns a tiny <span class="monospace">createRoot</span> and calls
      <span class="monospace">.update()</span>, so only that
      <span class="monospace">&lt;g&gt;</span> changes.
    </li>
    <li>
      Config widgets that still need React (colors, text, icons) live on an external
      <span class="monospace">reactiveState</span>. The UI subscribes with
      <span class="monospace">useSyncExternalStore</span>, so React re-renders only when
      that store notifies — not sixty times a second while you drag.
    </li>
  </ol>
</div>

